reveal:
3, not 1.

The default expression [] is evaluated ONCE, when the def statement runs - not on each call. That one list is stored on the function object (collect.__defaults__) and every call that omits the argument appends to it, so the three calls return 1, 2 and 3.

The fix is the None sentinel:

    def collect(item, bucket=None):
        if bucket is None:
            bucket = []
        bucket.append(item)
        return len(bucket)

Same trap for {} and set(), and for any default that captures state: datetime.now() as a default freezes the import-time clock into every call. It is a consequence of Python's evaluate-once rule, so it does not carry over blindly - JavaScript re-evaluates a default parameter on every call, and Java has no default arguments at all.
